  PROJECT 1: LLM Evaluation & Monitoring Framework

  Client-Facing Title: Production LLM Observatory — Eval, Drift Detection & Quality Monitoring

  Problem it Solves: Companies deploy LLMs and have no visibility into hallucination rates, response quality degradation, latency SLA breaches, or cost anomalies over time.

  Target Client Persona: Series A–C AI startups with LLM in production; enterprises adding AI to workflows; ML platform teams at mid-sized SaaS.

  Core Features:
  - Automated evaluation suite (LLM-as-judge + deterministic metrics)
  - Latency, token cost, and throughput dashboards
  - Hallucination/drift alerting (configurable thresholds)
  - Dataset versioning and regression testing
  - Slack/PagerDuty alert integration

  System Architecture:
  Requests → LLM Gateway (logging layer)
                ↓
           Eval Engine (LLM-as-judge + ROUGE/BLEU/semantic similarity)
                ↓
           Metrics Store (ClickHouse / SQLite for small scale)
                ↓
           Dashboard (Grafana / custom Streamlit)
                ↓
           Alert Manager → Slack / PagerDuty

  Tech Stack:
  - Python, FastAPI, LangSmith (or custom), OpenAI/Anthropic/local LLM
  - ClickHouse or DuckDB for metrics storage
  - Grafana or Streamlit for dashboards
  - Pytest + RAGAS for eval harness
  - Docker Compose for deployment

  Deployment Strategy: Docker Compose (self-hosted); optional cloud deploy on Railway/Render.

  Cost Awareness:
  - LLM-as-judge uses ~100–300 tokens/eval call; batch nightly to minimize cost
  - DuckDB/SQLite = zero infra cost at small scale
  - Offer both API-based and local Ollama judge options

  Differentiation Layer:

  ▎ 90% of freelancers set up logging. You deliver a complete eval loop with regression detection, cost attribution per feature/user, and drift alerting. This is what $40K/yr MLOps engineers get paid to build internally.

