  PROJECT 3: LLM Fine-tuning Pipeline (LoRA + Alignment)

  Client-Facing Title: Custom LLM Training Pipeline — Domain Adaptation with LoRA/QLoRA

  Problem it Solves: Generic LLMs produce generic outputs. Clients in legal, medical, finance, and customer service need models that match their terminology, tone, safety constraints, and domain knowledge without leaking proprietary data
  to external APIs.

  Target Client Persona: Enterprises with sensitive data (healthcare, legal, finance); SaaS companies building AI features with branded voice; companies hitting OpenAI API cost ceilings.

  Core Features:
  - Data preparation and cleaning pipeline (deduplication, quality filtering)
  - QLoRA fine-tuning with configurable hyperparameters
  - RLHF / DPO alignment layer (preference data collection + training)
  - Evaluation suite (before/after comparison on domain benchmarks)
  - Model export and serving (GGUF for local, vLLM for cloud)

  System Architecture:
  Raw Data (PDFs, JSONL, CSV, web)
      ↓
  Data Pipeline (cleaning → chunking → formatting → train/val split)
      ↓
  Fine-tuning Engine (HuggingFace Transformers + PEFT + QLoRA)
      ↓
  Alignment Layer (DPO on preference pairs)
      ↓
  Evaluation Harness (domain benchmark + LLM-as-judge)
      ↓
  Model Export → GGUF (local via llama.cpp) or vLLM endpoint

  Tech Stack:
  - Python, HuggingFace Transformers, PEFT, TRL
  - Axolotl or LLaMA-Factory (config-driven training)
  - Weights & Biases for experiment tracking
  - vLLM for serving; llama.cpp for local/GGUF
  - AWS/GCP spot instances for GPU training (cost-effective)

  Deployment Strategy: Training on cloud GPU (Lambda Labs / RunPod < $2/hr); serving on vLLM locally or cloud.

  Cost Awareness:
  - QLoRA 7B: ~$8–20 on RunPod A100 for 3 epochs
  - QLoRA 13B: ~$30–60
  - Show clients: cost comparison vs 6 months of GPT-4 API calls at scale

  Differentiation Layer:

  ▎ Beyond fine-tuning: you deliver data curation pipelines, alignment (DPO), evaluation harness, and serving — end-to-end. Most freelancers run one training script. You ship a repeatable pipeline that clients can re-run as their data
